00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:03.429 align:start position:0%
 
hi everyone my name is mao and i work  for dr jean-luc jannik at cornell today  i will share with you some unique  aspects of breeding for sugar kelp  zagrena lattissima  so kelp has mainly been used as a food  source for human consumption mostly in  asian countries lately in the u.s and in  the european countries more research are  interested in breeding kelp for biofuel  people also extract compounds from kelp  for pharmaceutical uses  the selective breeding on zagrana  japonica which is one type of kelp  started early in the 1940s in asian  countries  however kelp breeding in the u.s is  still at its infancy stage  in 2018  our collaborators went out to collect  wild samples from gulf of maine to  southern new england these two regions  are separated by cape cod kennel which  is known as the biogeographical barrier  for many other species we did find very  distinct subpopulation structure  between these two regions and the gulf  of maine population is the focus of  today's talk  kelp has a unique biphasic life cycle we  start from the sporophytes harvested  from ocean these are diploids and can be  phenotyped once sporophytes matures they  produce soros tissue  which is the reproductive tissue we  collect them let them induce spores in  the water these spores are free living  and floating around in the water which  is very different than row crop seeds we  have these spores go through flow  cytometry single cell sorting system  each spore then can develop into either  a female or male gametophytes  gametophytes are haploids we store each  gametophyte in a separate container and  this is our germplasm stock when  crossing we mix them and if  fertilization happens young sporophytes  will develop and then we spray them onto  seed string  four to five weeks later we transfer  them onto larger growth rope and plant  them on the farm  this is what it looks like with our  current breeding pipeline we start from  the wild spur of ice which is our  founder population from there we  generated this stock of gametophytes  which is of germplasm from that  germplasm we choose gametophytes to make  crosses and testing the progeny's  provides on farm  if by the time of harvesting these  sporophytes are mature and reproductive  we can then collect source tissue and  induce spores and to get the next  generation of gametophytes in the lab  this gametophyte takes at least a year  to grow and accumulate biomass in order  for us to make crosses for the next  generation so our current breeding  pipeline takes at least two years to  finish a breeding cycle  genomic selection happens for this  gametophyte stage where we genotype them  we use the field data from sporophytes  to build a model and predict the  breeding values for gametophytes so we  can pick the best gametophytes as  parents to make crosses  some pictures on how the pipeline looks  like here is when we collect a source  tissue clean them and then let them  induce spores in the lab in the water  once they're sorted the gametophytes can  happen and this shows gametophytes are  stored in flasks with sea water  and then when we cross we mix those tiny  amount of female and male gametophytes  mix them and then store back into flasks  this is after the mixture was already  sprayed onto the seed stream  then the seed string were transferred to  growing rope and ready to be planted in  the ocean  finally the crew is harvesting  we used a very typical augmented design  on the farm including line effects block  effects and ear effects using common  checks across different blocks once we  harvest this we would measure plot level  biomass traits as well as morphology  traits which indicates the blade length  width  thickness stipe length and type diameter  this is what it looks like  when we harvested the kelp we store them  in this huge container  and then we weigh each plot to get their  plot level wet weight  we then cut it to get subsamples  we also look for source tissue and store  them  for those individual blade measurements  we would keep the samples in the bags  first  and after we finish all the plot level  measurements we then go to each one of  those back and then start doing  individual measurements  so using these traits from sporophytes  we build genomic selection model and we  evaluated the accuracy between different  populations  in this case training populations are  data from year 2019 2020 and year 2021  it's used as the confirmation population  we also try to predict between 2019 and  2020.  so we use the pedigree and also marker  information to build a relationship  matrix the total individuals that's  included in this analysis are 950 so  this would include founders  gametophytes  sporophytes and the new generation of  gametophytes  we compared different models to test the  genomic selection accuracy the first one  is the base model g block model  we used both pedigree and markers  information the second one is general  combining ability and specific combining  ability model that's constructed in bglr  we compared using category plus markers  to construct the relationship matrix  versus only using the pedigree  in our case we have both pedigree and  markers estimated relationship matrix we  combine them using the actomir add-on  method in this figure for example he has  two pedigree matrix and one marker  estimated relationship matrix your  output is a combined kernel so in our  study we have pedigree estimated  relationship matrix for all the  individuals and then markers estimated  relationship matrix for the founders and  for the gametophytes we then combine all  of them using the package  here is trade heritability figure x-axis  represents different traits their  abbreviations are listed on the right  side y-axis is the trade heritability on  the left side of the dash bar here  represents biomass traits on the right  side represents morphology traits we do  see that morphology traits have higher  heritability than biomass traits the  first trait dry weight per meter which  is  the yield that we're interested in kelp  we do see that heritability for yield  trades varies from one year to another  which is also typical in row crops  and we had tested significant year  effects and genotype by environment  interaction  we then looked at the correlation among  these traits again above this dash bar  represents biomass trades below it  represents morphology traits the first  trait is dry weight per meter dry weight  perimeter is  significantly correlated with the force  trait which is blade density but not  with any of the morphology traits which  indicates if a blade is very small  within that plot those small blades may  grow denser and that in return  contribute to the weight  for genomic selection we first compare  different models on this figure x-axis  represents different models as i've  mentioned earlier y-axis is the gs  accuracy through cross-validation the  third one is general combining ability  and specific combining ability model  using both pedigree and markers  information it gives higher js accuracy  than the other two though the difference  is not significant  we then looked at the js prediction  accuracy between environments here  y-axis is the gs accuracy  and x-axis represents different traits  again we have biomass traits and  morphology traits except for blade  thickness morphology traits in general  gave higher just accuracy than biomass  trades between environments the first  one is the dry weight perimeter that's  what we are most interested in it had an  accuracy about 0.17 which is on the  lower range but it's still within the  range of gs accuracy for yield in row  crops we then added 2021 data as  confirmation set on this figure x-axis  represents different traits y-axis is  the gs accuracy different colors  represents training data set is from a  different year or years combination  again the gs accuracy varies and for the  first trade drive weight per meter is  around 0.2 it's still on the lower side  but that still falls within the range of  gs accuracy predicting between  environments for yield in row crops  many factors affect gs accuracy and we  assess the trade genetic correlation  between years we do see variation for  the same trade from one year to another  between year 2020 and 2021 which is in  gray bar more trades had larger genetic  correlation than the other year's  combinations this is likely due to that  year 2020 and year 2021 had similar  length of growing season which is longer  than year 2019  for dry weight per meter we estimated  almost zero phenotypic correlation  between years and so gs approach it's  still a little more predictive we do  think that the large year to year  environmental variation and g by e is  limiting our ability to selection  let's take a look at the breeding values  for dry weight per meter over  generations  x axis indicates 950 individuals each  dot represents each individual y-axis is  the breeding value for dry weight per  meter  different generations are shown by  different colors  black is the founder sporophytes green  other gametophytes from founders  these are also the parents of fun-grown  sporophytes which are in dark red dots  then we had a small amount of  gametophytes that are collected from  fungum sporophytes as shown in red  because gametophytes from those farm  tested sporophytes are collected at  random plots due to that soros tissue  ripened randomly on farm plots we do not  see any improvement of reading values  when you're comparing the red ones to  the green ones  although we did see some farm testes  profiles revealed some improvements as  they show higher breeding values than  the founder sporophytes  as you may have seen there are many  challenges that are unique to kelp  breeding compared to the row crops  in the past three seasons less than half  of the crosses we made generated useful  data the plot either did not grow or if  they grow but they become very patchy  for example you can see on the left side  of the plots that they grow somewhat  more uniformly than the plots on the  right side here we discard any data that  comes from a plot like those on the  right side  second there is a huge randomness on  whether or not soros tissue can ripen  when we harvest this would affect  whether or not we could select best  sporophytes to induce spores and produce  its next generation gametophytes  lastly  the gametophytes that we maintain in the  lab they grow really slow  in order to accumulate enough biomass  for making crosses it takes a lot of  efforts and time it's very labor  intensive to maintain those gametophytes  in the lab  we need to change seawater every week or  two and sometimes contamination could  also happen in the culture and then we  lose that gametophytes  here are some thoughts for next steps  we did a simulation study testing out  different scenarios in affecting our  gain of selection before we find that  high genetic gain could be achieved by  shortening our current breeding cycle  from two years to one year  we then tried a proof of concept  experiment this past season and  concluded that it is possible to  complete kelp life cycle within a year  on farm though we could only plant and  harvest a few of these individual  sporophytes  mostly still due to their parental  gametophytes biomass limitations  those sporophytes could not be tested as  meter long plots for yield but we could  incorporate them into our breeding  pipeline as common practices for early  stage nursery screening step  there are other breeding pipelines that  are worth assessing for example in order  to overcome the gametophyte's biomass  limitation we could make mixture plots  where multiple female and male  gametophytes are used much like in  forage breeding  our goal is to select communicates and  recommend to farmers in making hybrid  crosses  so far we do not have much data on  inbred lines though inbreeding  depression seemed very unlikely so we  need to continue our efforts in  developing inbred lines which could help  reduce labor from a long term as by then  we do not need the single cell sorting  step for the gametophytes  thank you for listening i'd like to  thank my advisors at cornell university  our collaborators at the university of  connecticut green wave bus hall ocean  graphic institution and our funding  source are part e welcome for questions  or comments and suggestions

